---
title: Who owns the DOM
summary: Every node inside a region has one owner. ogygia repairs, morphs and watches only what Svelte's hydration reads, and leaves a web component's own nodes alone. This page shows how to place a third-party widget so nothing fights over it.
related:
  - regions/client-islands
  - regions/lakes
  - app/server-transforms
---

An island's DOM does not belong to ogygia alone. Svelte binds parts of it when the island wakes, a web component rewrites its own light DOM when it upgrades, and a design-system script may strip whitespace while the island sleeps. To keep these from fighting, ogygia gives **every node one owner**, and everything ogygia does to the DOM starts by asking who owns the node in question. This matters most when you put a third-party widget, such as a web component or a vendor embed, inside an island, or when the devtools Hydration tab reports a **conflict**.

## The owners

| Owner                | What it is                                                                                                       | What ogygia does with it                                                         |
| -------------------- | ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Svelte's walk**    | the parts of the island's markup Svelte's hydration binds by position                                            | before the wake, puts it back to the server's shape if another script changed it |
| **Static**           | markup Svelte never reads again: `{@html}` content, `bind:innerHTML`, a custom element whose children are static | leaves it alone before the wake; a hole answer or a live tick still updates it   |
| **The page (yours)** | an element you marked `data-ogygia-keep`                                                                         | never touches it: not before the wake, not on a hole answer, not on a navigation |
| **A web component**  | children and attributes a custom element added itself                                                            | kept on a hole answer, a live tick and a navigation                              |
| **Lake**             | `wake: 'none'` content                                                                                           | the server's HTML, lifted out of the way while the island hydrates               |
| **Slot**             | host-page content an island adopts                                                                               | never repaired: Svelte adopts it whole                                           |

You don't mark static markup yourself. The compiler finds `{@html}`, `bind:innerHTML` and custom elements with static children, and stamps them in the server HTML with `data-og-opaque`. How ogygia restores markup that Svelte's walk owns is described in [Client islands](/docs/regions/client-islands#when-another-script-edits-a-sleeping-island).

## Placing a third-party widget

A widget that rewrites its own light DOM has to sit somewhere Svelte never reads. Any one of these placements works:

```svelte
<script>
  // 1. inside {@html}: Svelte takes the content whole and never walks into it
  const markup = `<vendor-search id="s"><form action="/search">…</form></vendor-search>`;
</script>

<div class="search">{@html markup}</div>

<!-- 2. a custom element whose children are static: Svelte never descends -->
<vendor-search><form action="/search"><input name="q" /></form></vendor-search>

<!-- 3. marked as yours: ogygia never touches it, anywhere -->
<vendor-search data-ogygia-keep="search">…</vendor-search>
```

Put the widget **inside** the `{@html}` content rather than making it the element that holds the content, because Svelte keeps its own markers as the direct children of that element. The `{@html}` may sit beside other content (`<div><h3>{title}</h3>{@html markup}</div>`), in which case the elements in that `<div>` are compared by position only and never entered. An `{@html}` at the very top of a component has no element around it to mark, so wrap it in one.

## Conflicts

If a web component changes its children in a place that Svelte's hydration **does** read, such as a custom element whose children use `{expressions}` or blocks, both cannot win. Svelte binds nodes by position, so ogygia removes the nodes the component added and lets the island hydrate. It reports the conflict instead of hiding it: you get a console warning in development and a **conflict** finding in devtools that names the element and the fix, which is one of the three placements above.

## What `data-ogygia-keep` means

On a plain element, `data-ogygia-keep` means that **the live DOM owns this subtree**. Before the wake, on a hole answer, on a live tick and on a navigation, ogygia matches the element and keeps it exactly as it is.

On an island, the `keep` import attribute means something related but different: the island survives a navigation. Svelte still owns that island, so it is repaired and hydrated like any other.
